WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.080
Jesse Harris, Filmmaker: Every single&nbsp;
character has to have something really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.080 --> 00:00:06.320
compelling that they're fighting&nbsp;
for or want they can't just be.

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:09.840
Film Courage: Who taught you&nbsp;
how to write a screenplay?

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:14.880
Jesse: Let's see I mean I don't think&nbsp;
any one person to me had every screenplay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:18.320
I definitely have read lots of the&nbsp;
screenwriting books and things that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:27.360
you would be told to read like save the cat or&nbsp;
all the other ones and I think those things are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:32.240
super helpful because knowing that structure is&nbsp;
really important the podcast like script notes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:36.160
think anything like that like I think it's just&nbsp;
as much of that information you could take in is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:41.040
is super important but I think at the end of the&nbsp;
day you just have to start writing and then you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:45.680
have to kind of figure it out and I think at least&nbsp;
for me because again that's how I really shine and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:51.200
learn is is just by doing it and so I had been&nbsp;
writing stories ever since I was a little kid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:56.720
I mean I made my first movie when I was eight&nbsp;
years old and so I had always been kind of writing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.720 --> 00:01:00.400
and telling stories and so I think it is something&nbsp;
I've been kind of working on my whole life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:06.480
and even when I wasn't making a film I was maybe&nbsp;
doing something else whether it's a commercial or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:12.400
this film festival I ran I was still in some way&nbsp;
kind of telling a story to an audience or or kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:19.600
of creating some sort of experience right and so&nbsp;
I think it's it's writing is one of those things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:24.720
you're probably like filmmaking is some element&nbsp;
that's just born in you right that has that in you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:29.200
and then it just takes a lot of time of&nbsp;
just doing it and getting practice doing it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:33.280
I mean I think the books and other resources&nbsp;
can give you a lot of inspiration and also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:37.920
really good at letting you then pick apart your&nbsp;
stuff and figure out what's not working in it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:40.880
but in the end of the day I think you&nbsp;
just have to kind of do it and kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:45.840
almost make yourself good by just by doing it&nbsp;
enough how do you start writing a screenplay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:57.840
like I mean me personally I start with the I guess&nbsp;
the big picture concept what is this about from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:05.200
there I would talk about I would think about who&nbsp;
are the characters what's going to be compelling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:11.280
and what are the stakes here and then I think from&nbsp;
there kind of trying to figure out the structure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:18.160
I mean obviously there's lots of structure to a&nbsp;
feature script not that it necessarily has to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:21.280
that but that's kind of how you usually write it&nbsp;
is kind of figuring out these acts and different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:25.760
points of the story right and I think that was&nbsp;
helpful for me with this feature just because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:30.880
Borrego is in a sense a kind of action movie&nbsp;
it is that kind of more of a commercial feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:34.960
even though it it has a lot more to it than&nbsp;
that but it was still helpful to kind of have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:40.080
those kind of maybe beats and things along&nbsp;
the way to kind of work towards and I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:42.960
that is super helpful to kind of know okay I&nbsp;
have this much for that and that much for that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:48.960
not to be stuck into that but it was helpful&nbsp;
to kind of give me something to work towards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:55.760
but I don't know I'm not an expert I don't think&nbsp;
there's one perfect way of writing a script but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.760 --> 00:03:01.600
I think really it is important to have what is&nbsp;
that really compelling idea and what is what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:05.600
is the really interesting things about each of&nbsp;
these characters that make you want to watch them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:09.120
and create a story and drama from because if&nbsp;
you don't have that I think you're really going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:14.160
to struggle when you get to actually physically&nbsp;
writing the words of every scene and so I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:19.760
now more than ever when I write something it's&nbsp;
kind of really doing as much diving into it before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:26.080
I even start writing to kind of make sure I have&nbsp;
that there would you see an Ellie or a Guillermo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:30.000
and kind of study them a real life and&nbsp;
just you come from afar and just envision&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:35.680
yeah that that and then try to incorporate some&nbsp;
of that into the script yeah I mean I'm definitely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:41.760
someone wherever I go I'm always watching people&nbsp;
and I definitely think I take a lot from from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:46.320
real life I mean I think that's one of the best&nbsp;
things about traveling too is wherever you are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:50.080
especially if it's somewhere out of your comfort&nbsp;
zone just seeing some little moment or something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:54.320
can give you so much inspiration I mean I'm always&nbsp;
writing down ideas and and little things I saw are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.320 --> 00:04:00.080
people I talked to or a conversation I heard I&nbsp;
think that's definitely a great way to I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:03.680
that's probably partly why you become a better&nbsp;
writer usually as you get older too is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:08.320
you just have more life experience you've we've&nbsp;
witnessed more characters you've had more stories&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:13.120
and I think that's something where as you're a&nbsp;
younger filmmaker you probably have to really pull&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:18.160
more from yourself or from your family or things&nbsp;
like that and find those connections because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:22.880
obviously there's just so many rich characters out&nbsp;
there in the real world that you can definitely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:27.840
pull from for sure yeah what about the&nbsp;
dialogue for these characters did you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:32.720
did it take time to refine it because I mean&nbsp;
they have a very interesting relationship I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:37.600
sure more in the midpoint or for sure yeah I mean&nbsp;
dialogue is definitely something that's that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:42.080
always really hard with with a script right is&nbsp;
to make that sound really authentic and real and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:47.600
for me when I write I really&nbsp;
kind of perform the scripts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:52.800
like I'm really not an actor but I need to&nbsp;
speak out loud when I write the script and so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:59.360
I'm really almost like acting out the movie as I&nbsp;
write it and so I think I actually do a lot of it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:03.360
before I even write it down I'll kind of be saying&nbsp;
it over and over again so I do spend a lot of time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:09.120
with the dialogue to kind of try to make sure it&nbsp;
sounds realistic and of course your first draft&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:11.920
is probably never going to be great but as you&nbsp;
go on that's really what changes probably more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:15.920
than anything is the dialogue I think the actual&nbsp;
structure of the film is really similar to what it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:21.200
was in the first draft but what's different is&nbsp;
other than some character probably development&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:25.840
expanding is a lot of the dialogue and just&nbsp;
continually working that to make that sound real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:30.240
and then that really also continues when you&nbsp;
go to make the movie I mean obviously some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:34.400
directors and writers are very strict about&nbsp;
their scripts but I was never like that where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:40.160
if the actor has another idea for a way to say&nbsp;
this that's great and especially in my case the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:44.960
film has several scenes in Spanish which I'm not&nbsp;
fluent in Spanish at all and so in those scenes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:48.720
especially it was very important for those actors&nbsp;
to kind of take ownership of that and be able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:53.520
say this in a way that was casual or slang or&nbsp;
however they would say that but even in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:58.080
english scenes like I mean I try to have each of&nbsp;
the actors be able to take something from their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:02.800
own life and bring it to that character and so&nbsp;
we did a lot of adjusting even before we shot the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:08.560
film of like what is this not something you would&nbsp;
do or how could we make this more relatable to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:13.280
you and hear some words we could change here and&nbsp;
there and you're even doing that on the fly while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.280 --> 00:06:18.400
making the movie like doing a take and that didn't&nbsp;
sound right like what else could we try and so I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:23.360
think to get really believable strong dialogue&nbsp;
it's just always about experimenting and and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:27.680
kind of tinkering with it I guess that's&nbsp;
that's really at least how I approach it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:33.200
what makes your writing process different from&nbsp;
everyone else I mean I can't speak for too many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:39.440
other people because I don't know that many other&nbsp;
people's writing processes but I mean mine has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:45.600
been evolving I guess I mean I haven't written&nbsp;
that many things right and so it's it's kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:50.080
of constantly learning I guess what works&nbsp;
I think the thing I just recently have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:56.000
writing is this limited series that I've&nbsp;
been developing and I think for me it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.000 --> 00:07:00.080
it was trying to take as much lessons as I learned&nbsp;
from the first the last script which is just that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.640 --> 00:07:05.360
the characters really have to have every single&nbsp;
character has to have something really compelling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:11.600
that they're fighting for or want they can't just&nbsp;
be existing they really even if we don't know in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.600 --> 00:07:16.560
the story hear every detail of it I have to kind&nbsp;
of know that exists and have it in my head so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.360 --> 00:07:23.360
I most recently kind of wrote a whole page or two&nbsp;
on every character and what was their backstory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:27.280
and what was their life like and all these things&nbsp;
that are maybe they won't even make it into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:34.160
script but I think I'm just doing as much kind of&nbsp;
pre-thinking and planning as I can now where I'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.160 --> 00:07:39.600
just be kind of meditating on it for a long time&nbsp;
and I'll get an idea and I'll take a voice note or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:44.800
something and I'll record that and i'd spend a lot&nbsp;
of time thinking about it before I even started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.800 --> 00:07:49.040
writing it and at least for me that's I think what&nbsp;
turned into a really one is it was a lot easier to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.040 --> 00:07:52.880
actually write it when I did write it and then&nbsp;
two I think it's the strongest writing I've ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.880 --> 00:07:58.400
had and so I think the more time you can kind of&nbsp;
just let it all sink in I think does allow it to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.400 --> 00:08:05.280
kind of marinate and kind of be even stronger in&nbsp;
the script for Borrego that's like a three-year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.840 --> 00:08:11.200
marination I well for Borrego I think it was&nbsp;
it was slightly different I probably more in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.200 --> 00:08:16.960
that sense diving quicker with the first draft and&nbsp;
then spent a lot more time adjusting and changing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.960 --> 00:08:22.080
and refining whereas the thing I've more even&nbsp;
more recently written that hasn't been made yet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.200 --> 00:08:27.360
I probably spent more time marinating but then&nbsp;
less time actually writing to get something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:33.840
really strong so I don't know if there's one&nbsp;
perfect way or anything but at least for me I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.840 --> 00:08:37.760
think that's what I found to make something really&nbsp;
compelling is if I spend a little bit more time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.760 --> 00:08:43.120
just kind of letting it all sink in before diving&nbsp;
in to write it what was it about Borrego that just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.120 --> 00:08:51.520
you couldn't stop writing I think there was just&nbsp;
this personal connection for me with the film both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.520 --> 00:08:55.440
with this this idea that started with my dad&nbsp;
based on kind of him as this amateur botanist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.560 --> 00:09:01.600
but then two just the desert itself was a&nbsp;
character of this movie really was like the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.600 --> 00:09:06.480
fifth or sixth character of the movie was this&nbsp;
landscape and was this desert and I think just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:11.440
having grown up I spent so much of my childhood&nbsp;
camping and going on these desert adventures with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.440 --> 00:09:15.920
my parents that was just where they went we didn't&nbsp;
go to like really Disneyland or other places we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.920 --> 00:09:20.480
did once but mostly we went to the desert and I&nbsp;
wandered around the desert and thought of ideas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.480 --> 00:09:27.360
and was just in that kind of world and so I think&nbsp;
I just knew this was such a compelling setting and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.360 --> 00:09:32.080
so few things had kind of been set specifically&nbsp;
in this desert almost nothing had been shot there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.640 --> 00:09:39.200
and I just loved that kind of movie this is almost&nbsp;
like a neo-western of sorts it doesn't it doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:43.760
really feel so much like a western but it kind of&nbsp;
has a lot of that vibes and I do like that kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.760 --> 00:09:48.400
film and so I just knew this was gonna be a good&nbsp;
film I knew it was really important to make a film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.400 --> 00:09:51.760
that I wanted to make a film that people wanted&nbsp;
to see I didn't want to just make a film that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.720 --> 00:09:57.600
too intimate and it would never get seen I wanted&nbsp;
to kind of make this blending of worlds where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.600 --> 00:10:02.080
it had this kind of commercial appeal with&nbsp;
some action and excitement and thrills but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.080 --> 00:10:08.480
also wasn't just that fluff either it had dynamic&nbsp;
characters that were interesting and authentic and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:12.880
was something that kind of this blending&nbsp;
of films that maybe doesn't exist as much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.880 --> 00:10:17.360
and I just knew that if I could pull that off I&nbsp;
could make something really really interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.360 --> 00:10:22.880
that people would want to see do you believe that&nbsp;
there are writing dead ends or that a writer can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.880 --> 00:10:29.280
always find a creative solution out of sort&nbsp;
of a what seems like they can't go any further

00:10:31.360 --> 00:10:35.440
I think definitely you could you could reach&nbsp;
a dead end if you didn't probably do enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.440 --> 00:10:40.640
thinking ahead of time and and kind of figuring&nbsp;
out if this idea was going to work perhaps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.240 --> 00:10:46.160
I've never not finished a screenplay or something&nbsp;
but I know a lot of people do struggle with that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.160 --> 00:10:50.240
where they get to a point they can't keep writing&nbsp;
but I think that's probably just because you you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.240 --> 00:10:54.000
probably started writing too soon before you&nbsp;
really figured out what is this character's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.960 --> 00:10:59.040
I don't say motivation but kind of all&nbsp;
those kind of typical things like what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.040 --> 00:11:04.480
those are real like what what what is it this&nbsp;
movie's really about and what is it this person's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.480 --> 00:11:09.840
fighting for whatever kind of genre the film is&nbsp;
of course you can reach those kind of temporary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.840 --> 00:11:13.840
dead ends probably all the time struggling&nbsp;
with writer's block and all that kind of stuff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.800 --> 00:11:19.440
but I think in terms of ones that you can't&nbsp;
get out of it's probably started because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.440 --> 00:11:23.040
there's something missing in your kind&nbsp;
of development of the overall concept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.920 --> 00:11:28.720
did you have any troubles with Borrego let's&nbsp;
say the second act or the ending anything where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.720 --> 00:11:35.920
it was just a little bit more time spent on it&nbsp;
yeah for sure I mean I think the second act is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.920 --> 00:11:41.280
always the hardest just because the first act&nbsp;
is just setting up you're kind of even if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.280 --> 00:11:45.680
don't have the idea figured out you can kind&nbsp;
of set that story up and get it going right and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.680 --> 00:11:49.520
the ending you hopefully know the ending or where&nbsp;
it's going and the stuff in the middle is usually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.520 --> 00:11:54.000
what's the hardest because it's longer usually and&nbsp;
it's what has to kind of keep the momentum going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.000 --> 00:12:01.680
and so I think that's probably where I had the&nbsp;
most challenge of kind of figuring that out and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.600 --> 00:12:08.000
I think also the some challenges I had is as I&nbsp;
got into trying to actually finance this film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.560 --> 00:12:13.360
I did have to start cutting back certain things&nbsp;
in order to make the budget work so I had to start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.360 --> 00:12:19.360
cutting some scenes and trimming some areas and so&nbsp;
then it kind of came more like a bit of a puzzle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.360 --> 00:12:24.400
of like okay what could I take away that won't&nbsp;
hurt the film but it will make us a cheaper film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.400 --> 00:12:29.360
to be able to make and so I think it's always this&nbsp;
kind of constant kind of journey with the script&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.360 --> 00:12:35.120
especially if you're making the script to direct&nbsp;
it never is this perfect linear process I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.320 --> 00:12:40.320
what makes us care about the characters&nbsp;
in Borrego so I think in Borrego&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.320 --> 00:12:43.440
we care about the characters because&nbsp;
they all have something that they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.440 --> 00:12:50.560
they're kind of fighting for or dreaming towards&nbsp;
so even the we'll say bad guys in the Borrego&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.080 --> 00:12:57.200
they're not really evil people they&nbsp;
they've gotten into a situation that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.960 --> 00:13:06.240
they they they didn't necessarily want to be&nbsp;
in but it's where they are so in one case they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.240 --> 00:13:10.560
needed to support their family and so they took&nbsp;
a job doing something they knew that was wrong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.560 --> 00:13:18.000
or illegal but it was their only choice or someone&nbsp;
is running away from something in their their past&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.000 --> 00:13:23.280
and they're trying to put it behind them but they&nbsp;
know it's going to catch up with them but they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.280 --> 00:13:26.800
just ignoring that and I think there's something&nbsp;
that we can all relate to in all these characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.680 --> 00:13:31.280
most of us aren't drug dealers and&nbsp;
crossing the border and all that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.280 --> 00:13:37.120
but you can still find the humanity in these&nbsp;
characters and and that was something that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.120 --> 00:13:41.840
super important to me because I really wanted&nbsp;
to be super sensitive to making a film about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.840 --> 00:13:47.280
these kind of people and and this border story&nbsp;
because I'm not from that world I don't live that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.280 --> 00:13:51.760
I have lots of friends who have family members who&nbsp;
are from that and could talk to them about that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.760 --> 00:13:57.120
but I really wanted to try to tell this in as I&nbsp;
guess gentle and authentic as a way possible where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.120 --> 00:14:01.520
it wasn't really about that the film sets&nbsp;
that up and that's what sets up the story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.520 --> 00:14:06.800
but ultimately it's about just these individual&nbsp;
people and what their choices are and what their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.800 --> 00:14:11.840
beliefs are and what they're fighting for&nbsp;
in their own life and that's really what the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.840 --> 00:14:21.040
film is about so I think that's what makes it&nbsp;
unique and and compelling for people to watch.

